{
	# Sites' on-demand TLS ask endpoint — must be the file's first block (Caddyfile global options).
	# The "ask" directive alone is not sufficient; the sites site block below still needs its own
	# tls { on_demand } to opt in (docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section has the verified
	# syntax and why both pieces are required together).
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://api:8080/v1/sites/_ask-tls
	}

	# Global default issuer — required, not optional, and the *only* place this can correctly live.
	# Found live 2026-08-25: a previous fix tried a per-site `tls { on_demand; issuer acme }` on each
	# of three site blocks (a `*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}` block, a `*.*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}` block,
	# and this file's bare `https://` catch-all). That looked correct in `caddy adapt`'s static output
	# (each block's automation policy showed an explicit acme issuer) but was STILL silently issuing
	# self-signed internal certs for real site hostnames at runtime. Root cause, confirmed by reading
	# Caddy v2.11.4's actual source (not docs) plus a live throwaway reproduction: Caddy's Automatic
	# HTTPS logic rebuilds automation policies at startup from each site block's own address, assigning
	# the address itself as that policy's `subjects` — and does NOT carry forward a per-site `issuer`
	# directive into that rebuilt policy (confirmed via the running config, not just the adapted static
	# file — Caddy's own `autosave.json`/debug "adjusted config" log showed the issuer silently absent
	# after this rebuild, for every per-site `issuer acme`, including the fully-spelled-out
	# `issuer acme { dir https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory }` form). With no issuer left on
	# the policy, Caddy falls back to `DefaultIssuersProvisioned()`, whose own heuristic is "internal
	# unless every subject qualifies for a public cert" (modules/caddytls/automation.go) — and
	# certmagic's `SubjectQualifiesForPublicCert` (certificates.go) explicitly rejects any subject with
	# more than one wildcard label, which is exactly what a two-or-three-label wildcard site *address*
	# like `*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}` looks like once it becomes the policy's own subject. So the more
	# wildcard-specific a site block's address was, the more certain it was to fail this check and fall
	# back to the internal CA — regardless of any issuer written on that same block.
	#
	# The fix has two parts, verified together against a live throwaway Caddy instance with the exact
	# production Caddyfile shape: (1) delete the per-site wildcard blocks entirely — see the comment on
	# the single site block below for why they're not needed, since Praxy's own hostname dispatch
	# doesn't depend on Caddy having per-depth site blocks; (2) set the issuer here, globally, via
	# `cert_issuer` — a policy assembled by Caddy's own Automatic HTTPS with genuinely EMPTY `subjects`
	# (which is what a single host-less `https://` block collapses to) does NOT hit the multi-wildcard
	# rejection above, and correctly reaches this global default. Verified live: with this shape,
	# `docker compose logs caddy` shows a genuine ACME order against `acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org` for
	# every hostname depth tested (a two-label site hostname, a three-label preview hostname, and a
	# bare custom domain) — proven via Let's Encrypt's own rejection of throwaway `.invalid` test
	# domains ("not a valid public suffix"), which only happens after a real order attempt, not an
	# instant internal-CA substitution. See docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section for the full
	# transcript, including why the earlier per-site `issuer acme` fix looked right and wasn't.
	#
	# `cert_issuer` applies globally, not just to the on-demand catch-all below — it's also now what
	# issues the console/API's own static certificate (the `{$PRAXY_DOMAIN} {$PRAXY_CONSOLE_DOMAIN}`
	# block). Checked whether this narrows that block's issuer choice, since Caddy's zero-config
	# default (`DefaultIssuersProvisioned()`) can include ZeroSSL as an automatic Let's-Encrypt
	# fallback: it can't here, and never could — that fallback is only appended when a global `email`
	# option is set (`DefaultIssuers`'s own source, `strings.TrimSpace(userEmail) != ""`), and this
	# Caddyfile has never set one. So the static block was already Let's-Encrypt-only before this
	# line existed; adding `cert_issuer acme` here is explicit, not a narrowing.
	cert_issuer acme
}

{$PRAXY_DOMAIN} {$PRAXY_CONSOLE_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# Every hosted site's public hostname: subdomains (`<key>.<projectId>.{PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}`), preview
# URLs (`<deploymentId>.<key>.<projectId>.{PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}`), and custom domains a site owner points
# at this box (Sites Phase 3) all land on this one block. Earlier revisions of this file had three
# separate blocks here — two wildcard-depth-specific ones (`*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}`,
# `*.*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}`) plus this catch-all — because Caddy's on-demand automation-policy
# matching was found (Sites Phase 1/2) to be as strict about wildcard depth as a real TLS wildcard cert:
# a single `*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}` block only matched one label and silently refused on-demand
# issuance for anything deeper. That's still true of depth-*specific* wildcard blocks — but is a
# non-issue for a single host-less block like this one, which has no depth restriction to violate in
# the first place and so correctly catches every hostname shape uniformly, confirmed live (see the
# `cert_issuer` comment above for the fuller story of why the three-block version actually had to go:
# each per-site wildcard block's own address became its automation policy's `subjects`, and Caddy
# rejects multi-label-wildcard subjects as ineligible for a public cert, silently downgrading those
# specific blocks to the internal CA no matter what issuer was written on them). Caddy does not need
# per-site awareness beyond this one block regardless of how many sites exist — api dispatches
# Host -> container internally (Praxy.Sites.SiteProxyMiddleware). Each distinct hostname still gets its
# own certificate issued lazily on first request, not one real wildcard cert (on-demand TLS's whole
# point: no DNS-01, no DNS-provider credentials). `https://` with the host portion omitted is Caddy's
# own documented catch-all address (verbatim from caddyserver.com/on-demand-tls's own worked example for
# this exact "many dynamically-created domains" shape, and the pattern a Caddy maintainer recommends in
# caddyserver/caddy#6518 for the same "on-demand + multiple wildcards" failure mode this file hit).
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}
